#edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049421 #edb/ref 1033 1941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049421 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "f3745544-3239-55ba-94eb-3deb858524c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049809 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049809 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049809 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049809 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049809 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428468 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049809 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428469 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049809 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428470 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049809 #edb/ref 1031 "Linearization of Multivariable Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049809 #edb/ref 1033 1937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049809 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "156d2779-eafb-5c80-bd7a-1ed2ab0b5ca6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428471 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428472 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1031 "The Gradient as a Normal Vector" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1033 1939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "26b9f4f0-7964-52b3-9a23-d31ae6927cb5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050143 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050143 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050143 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050143 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050143 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050143 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050143 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050143 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050143 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050143 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428464 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050143 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428465 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050143 #edb/ref 1031 "The Gradient Vector" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050143 #edb/ref 1033 1934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050143 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "3055ed78-44c0-5be7-9e08-cb26d881941a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050555 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050555 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050555 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050555 #edb/ref 1031 "Directional Derivatives" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050555 #edb/ref 1033 1938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050555 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "52ee4eec-32eb-50f8-908d-64e67d09f541" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124247 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3131" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124247 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\nabla f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,\\pi)$ given that $f(x,y) = (x - y)\\cos y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124247 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "526b792b-9952-5dae-bcd6-15564c0e013f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124247 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x-y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-(x-y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\text{ }i-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+(x-y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the gradient at the specified point, we substitute $(x,y)= (1,\\pi)$ into the above, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(1,\\pi ) & ={f}_{x}(1,\\pi )\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}(1,\\pi )\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\text{ }i-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +(1-\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124248 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3132" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124248 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate $\\nabla g(x,y,z)$   at $(2,-1,3)$ if $g(x,y,z) = x^2 + xyz.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124248 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bfade25e-065a-5c66-a554-9ee4e9559a55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124248 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+xyz) \\\\  & =2x+yz \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+xyz) \\\\  & =0+xz \\\\  & =xz \\\\ {g}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+xyz) \\\\  & =0+xy \\\\  & =xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & ={g}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }i+{g}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{g}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(2x+yz)\\text{ }i+(xz)\\text{ }j+(xy)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇g(2,-1,3) & =(2(2)+(-1)(3))\\text{ }i+2(3)\\text{ }j+2(-1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =1\\text{ }i+6\\text{ }j-2\\text{ }k \\\\  & =i+6\\text{ }j-2\\text{ }k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124249 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3133" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124249 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x,y)= (y-4)\\ln (x-2),$ find the points $(x, y)$ where $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124249 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b2549d7b-07c2-5f2f-89a3-2cccecf2ba22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124249 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((y-4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{y-4}{x-2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y-4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac {y-4}{x-2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x-2) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {y-4}{x-2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x-2) \\, \\mathbf j = 0 \\  \\mathbf i + 0\\ \\mathbf j\n$$\n\nBy equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ on both sides of the equation (and similarly with $\\mathbf{j}$), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-4}{x-2}=0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y-4=0,$ which means $y=4.$  From the second equation, we obtain $\\ln(x-2)=0,$ which means that $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(x,y) =\\mathbf  0$ at the point $(3,4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124251 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3150" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124251 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y) g(x,y)$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =xy,\\, &  & ∇f(x,y)=y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j, &  &  \\\\ g(x,y) & =\\sqrt{xy},\\, &  & ∇g(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2y}\\text{ }j. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124251 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "67e9bcb5-4bf2-56b8-8d82-95355841eaaa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124251 #edb/ref 1074 "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\nabla [fg] = g \\, \\nabla\\! f  + f \\, \\nabla\\! g.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule shown above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =g(x,y)∇f(x,y)+f(x,y)∇g(x,y) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{xy}(y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j)+xy(\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2y}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{xy}(y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j)+\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2}(y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{xy}}{2}(y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124443 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124443 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124443 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124443 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124443 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124443 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124443 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124443 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124443 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124443 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124443 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186437017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124443 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186437019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124443 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Gradient of a Function of Two Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124443 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124443 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e3991a54-342b-5cd0-87e2-606146240555" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124444 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124444 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124444 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124444 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124444 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124444 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124444 #edb/ref 1038 #e