ref 17592186262920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124447 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124447 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124447 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124447 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124447 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124447 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186437018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124447 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Gradient Using the Properties of the Gradient" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124447 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124447 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "67cfec73-130c-5b68-a2e0-5f12837bfc81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124727 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124727 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124727 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124727 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124727 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124727 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124727 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124727 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124727 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124727 #edb/ref 1045 "The Gradient Vector" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124727 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124727 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124727 #edb/ref 1050 1140.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124727 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "55f1d2f4-1d80-580f-ae6f-7bce48469649" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125020 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125020 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36918" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125020 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125020 #edb/ref 1071 "Given $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{xyz}$, find $∇f(x,y,z).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125020 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4903eb08-1095-52c0-bd3a-a8d7c39455f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125020 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{xyz}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot (yz) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2x} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{xyz}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot (xz) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2y} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{xyz}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot (xy) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2z}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2}(\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{z}\\text{ }k)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125274 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125274 #edb/ref 1061 10570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125274 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "be8dc2ef-0d3b-53c7-a3c2-8d5efed1d5c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125274 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125275 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125275 #edb/ref 1061 10571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125275 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bc0272c3-579a-51cf-8ff7-4a2addd6c160" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125275 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125276 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125276 #edb/ref 1061 10574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125276 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a7dd6ff5-617a-5a73-a336-1505e52faa0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125276 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125277 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125277 #edb/ref 1061 10575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125277 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d6afbd23-ca7a-5b4d-ba2e-965bf065da01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125277 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125278 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125278 #edb/ref 1061 10578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125278 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ecb77e79-95bc-5d3f-bdaa-68737214a697" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125278 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125279 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125279 #edb/ref 1061 10581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125279 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d4e8b345-fc40-5dfb-ab00-d4b25041d02c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125279 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125281 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125281 #edb/ref 1061 10701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125281 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "acd8b544-63b3-5271-9729-c95afd0bee1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125281 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125462 #edb/ref 1061 22631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125462 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "900dafcb-5560-5f91-8fbe-4fbd70dce61e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125462 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125726 #edb/ref 1064 "The **gradient** of a multivariable function is analogous to the derivative of a single-variable function.\n\nFor a function $f(x,y)$ whose partial derivatives exist at a point $(x,y),$ the **gradient vector** is denoted $\\nabla f(x,y)$ and can be found by computing the vector of partial derivatives:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\nabla f (x,y) =  \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\mathbf i + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\mathbf j\n                              \n}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125726 #edb/ref 1065 2399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125726 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4b8c5c84-83d9-55aa-bb25-87cf1904ed2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125726 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125727 #edb/ref 1064 "For a function of three variables $f(x,y,z)$, the gradient of $f$ is given by the vector  \n\n \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\nabla \\! f (x,y,z) &= f_x(x,y,z)\\ \\mathbf i + f_y (x,y,z)\\ \\mathbf j + f_z (x,y,z)\\ \\mathbf k.\n                             \n}\n$$\n\n \n\nFor a function $f(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n)$ of $n$ variables, its gradient is usually given as a column vector:\n\n![](images/21/21e05d85506da576e293bce86b1ca98f4f5b101c49b0591a49d80d2a4b8bfcc1.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125727 #edb/ref 1065 2400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125727 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "be2e3466-803d-5c6c-aeb9-f6cc57b000f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125727 #edb/ref 1067 "The Gradient of a Function of More Than Two Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125728 #edb/ref 1064 "Since the gradient of a function of several variables is made up of partial derivatives, we can expect it to have properties that are similar to partial derivatives.\n\nAssume that $f$ and $g$ are functions whose first-order partial derivatives exist and are continuous on some open domain, and that $a,b,$ and $c$ are real constants. Then, the gradient has the following properties:\n\n- Constant rule:  $\\nabla c = \\mathbf 0$\n\n- Linearity: $\\nabla (af + bg) = a \\nabla\\! f + b\\nabla\\! g$\n\n- Product Rule: $\\nabla(fg) = g \\nabla\\! f + f \\nabla\\! g$\n\n- Quotient Rule: $\\nabla \\left( \\dfrac{f}{g}\\right) = \\dfrac{ g \\nabla\\! f - f \\nabla\\! g}{g^2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125728 #edb/ref 1065 2401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125728 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "52036b8f-4d6f-5609-bbcf-fc34a51d9b98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125728 #edb/ref 1067 "Properties of the Gradient" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125806 #edb/ref 1064 "For a multivariable function, the existence of the gradient implies continuity.\n\nA sufficient condition for the gradient ![](images/3a/3a172b4fd0180b172579ebac50f73d8dfb75a219a02b610f666d1a4e446592a2.svg) to exist is that the partial derivatives ![](images/f2/f20807159d7e3d172a1ed9d92b8001a4c186e78ef3b8423592c75e44b633ec88.svg) and ![](images/f4/f423cce200533b35d0f6df2d4a6f5e917529276e749a7ab4995930d64559c98d.svg) both exist in a neighborhood of ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) and are continuous at ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg).\n\nTherefore, if ![](images/f2/f20807159d7e3d172a1ed9d92b8001a4c186e78ef3b8423592c75e44b633ec88.svg) and ![](images/f4/f423cce200533b35d0f6df2d4a6f5e917529276e749a7ab4995930d64559c98d.svg) both exist in a neighborhood of ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) and are continuous at ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg), then ![](images/bc/bc562d54615dad55d095db2afed7626f62ff7ebf0ab6c5d45471b77ac30b6398.svg) is continuous at ![](images/85/853e57b0bf9a8f7c16e59d37575ae23ac4028ed66b30720af9d7d49ab595c91b.svg)\n\n**Watch out!** The continuity of the partial derivatives is crucial. The existence of the partial derivatives alone does not imply that a function is continuous at a point." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125806 #edb/ref 1065 3864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125806 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "652f49b9-1a78-58d1-9531-1e115634f86a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125806 #edb/ref 1067 "Continuous Partial Derivatives Imply Continuity" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321558 #edb/ref 1080 "\\sqrt{xyz}(x\\text{ }i+y\\text{ }j+z\\text{ }k)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321558 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321558 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "35a99596-2194-56d8-b34a-8cf33e24d509" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322014 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{xyz}{2}(\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{z}\\text{ }k)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322014 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322014 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6fa3bf93-c16d-5b28-921e-41079fe50f98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322622 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2}(\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{z}\\text{ }k)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322622 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322622 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bdfd3cdb-b6de-5b57-8bc2-55f6cfdc0358" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322758 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2}(x\\text{ }i+y\\text{ }j+z\\text{ }k)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322758 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322758 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ce59c82e-5d51-5485-be48-7fb4d8c385b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322890 #edb/ref 1080 "\\sqrt{xyz}(\\frac{1}{yz}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{xz}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{xz}\\text{ }k)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322890 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322890 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "def79f1e-967d-536d-901e-8b6506e4e5d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323514 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186321558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323514 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323514 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323514 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323514 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323514 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323514 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323514 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186322622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323514 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b1682cef-8ea8-5e2a-8b48-c4bffce5ce2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333143 #edb/ref 1080 "y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+2)\\text{ }i+x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+2)\\text{ }j" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333143 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333143 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "10ef7585-f490-52ce-84d4-c14afbd71f8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333290 #edb/ref 1080 "y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+1)\\text{ }i+x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+1)\\text{ }j" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333290 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333290 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "21b12eb3-67b9-5f76-80f9-150884fc0d5e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333580 #edb/ref 1080 "x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i-y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }j" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333580 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333580 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3ea7c594-0a56-5e0b-a1fd-7fd5413db99e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335176 #edb/ref 1080 "x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }j" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335176 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335176 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f9a4ec80-da82-5689-8ecb-e79e98294b6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335228 #edb/ref 1080 "y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }j" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335228 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335228 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fe69f0a9-435b-5c17-9a63-9c54c8b0d0ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335609 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335609 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335609 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335609 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186335176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335609 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186335228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335609 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335609 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335609 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186333290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335609 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c8f1d221-bfab-57b4-bd66-7eea20107505" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335800 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335800 #edb/ref 1069 "q-65131" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335800 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335800 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =xy,\\, &  & ∇f(x,y)=y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j, &  &  \\\\ g(x,y) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy),\\, &  & ∇g(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335800 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "838d032d-5600-5cb9-9af4-36cc70864ee1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335800 #edb/ref 1074 "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=g\\,∇\\,f+f\\,∇\\,g\n$$\n\nSo, using this product rule we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =g(x,y)∇f(x,y)+f(x,y)∇g(x,y) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))(y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j)+xy(\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }j+y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j \\\\  & =[y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+y\\text{ }i]+[x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }j+x\\text{ }j] \\\\  & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+1)\\text{ }i+x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+1)\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428464 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428464 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1934->1929. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428464 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186048707 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428465 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428465 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account multivariable: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: multivariable=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/multivariable-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1934->1930. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428465 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186048942 #edb/ref 1759218604